---
/**
 * The idea behind the studio, stated plainly and bluntly. Leads with the outcome
 * (work that stands out and lasts), never the tooling. The brutalist motif
 * carries it: sameness is the risk these clients are paying to avoid.
 */
---

<section class="r-section r-manifesto">
  <p class="r-label" data-r-fade>01 / The idea</p>

  <h2 class="r-statement r-display r-glitch" data-r-lines>
    <span class="r-mask"><span class="r-mask-inner">Default is</span></span>
    <span class="r-mask"><span class="r-mask-inner">the enemy.</span></span>
  </h2>

  <div class="r-manifesto-cols">
    <p data-r-fade>
      Most of the web looks the same because it is built the same: templates,
      stock parts, safe defaults. For a studio, a label, or a tool with a point
      of view, that sameness is the real risk. Blending in is how good work goes
      unseen.
    </p>
    <p data-r-fade>
      We build from the structure out. Raw grids, honest type, motion with
      intent. Fast and accessible by default, unmistakably yours, made to be
      remembered and to hold up under real traffic long after launch.
    </p>
  </div>

  <span class="r-rule" data-r-rule aria-hidden="true"></span>
</section>

<style>
  .r-manifesto-cols {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: clamp(1.6rem, 4vw, 4rem);
    max-width: 920px;
    margin-bottom: clamp(2.6rem, 7vh, 4rem);
  }
  .r-manifesto-cols p {
    max-width: 46ch;
    font-size: clamp(0.96rem, 1.2vw, 1.08rem);
    line-height: 1.72;
    color: var(--r-muted);
  }
  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .r-manifesto-cols {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
